le raising the  possibility of holiday service problems  southwest says it now plans to allow  employees with approved medical or  religious reasons for avoiding  vaccinations to continue working  [Music]  one of the biggest companies staffing up  this holiday season is the united parcel  service or ups which said it's looking  to hire more than 100 000 people  record-breaking levels of packages are  expected this year shipping companies  like fedex and ups are frantically  searching for help right now we are  looking to hire  drivers both tractor trailer drivers and  local delivery drivers  we're also looking to hire part-time  package handlers and that would be the  largest number of people we're looking  for  to help attract those seasonal workers  ups streamlined its application process  so the company now responds to  applicants with offers within 30 minutes  of submitting the application  in the past applicants wouldn't hear  back from ups for about two weeks it's  been extremely successful in fact  they  survey all of our applicants and they  ask them how the process was  and the numbers went through the roof in  terms of positive  feelings about that whole process they  pivoted towards a digital approach to  hiring people  70 or more of all of our hires are done  on a cell phone  and we were nowhere near that three  years ago ups has also revamped its  outreach strategy to potential employees  we're trying to find people where they  live we're trying to find people where  they spend their time well that's these  days that's on social media between 70  and 75 percent  of our advertising spend  today is done on those social channels  versus  about five years ago when 70  to 75 was done on traditional channels  like print  and broadcast advertising  so it's a complete  flip-flop if you will of where we're  spending our money and how we're trying  to reach people  after the financial crisis of 2007 and  2008 led to a rapid increase in  unemployment many employers had their  pick of qualified job candidates so  after the great recession many employers  inflated the requirements needed for  jobs asking for example for bachelor's  degrees for it help desk specialists or  construction supervisors jobs that were  historically held by high school  graduates  this this left many non-degree holders  with few options besides low-paid  service jobs that didn't offer as much  career or wage growth  but today this decade plus period of  qualification inflation is beginning to  reverse as employers emphasize speed  over credentials to adapt to a  historically tight job market  last year 1.5 million jobs were closed  to people without a college degree but  since then the percentage of employers  that require a college degree dropped  from just over 20 percent in january  2019 to about 15 this october  many workforce specialists and employers  have said that those requirements often  weren't necessary some companies have  looked for example whether asking about  gpa has any impact on their performance  and many have found that it doesn't  economists say that these changes could  also narrow the gap between the 2.5  unemployment rate of college graduates  and the 5.8 rate of job seekers with a  high school diploma so right now there's  a change afoot there's a movement  towards more skill-based hiring looking  at potential rather than degrees and  many economists say this could outlast  the current hiring emergency many think  we'll see more of a movement towards  looking at skills rather than someone's  past experience or degrees  [Music]
 

